cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  DeMeyer_etal_2026_Extreme_Cyclones: analysis code for
  extratropical-cyclone-associated precipitation and near-surface
  wind extremes over North America
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite both the software itself
  and the associated article.
type: software
abstract: >-
  Analysis code of De Meyer et al. (2026). Extratropical cyclones (ETCs)
  are tracked in hourly ERA5 and CRCM6-GEM5 output over North America,
  tropical cyclones and transition phases are removed with the cyclone
  phase space, and the precipitation and near-surface wind exceedances
  above the 99th all-hour percentile within 1000 km of ETC centres are
  accumulated and decomposed into intensity, threshold and occurrence
  terms. The code evaluates the historical simulations (1980-2014)
  against ERA5 and, for precipitation, against IMERG (2001-2014),
  separates the contributions of the regional model and of the driving
  data, tests the significance of the differences, and attributes the
  projected changes (2063-2097, SSP3-7.0) of the individual ETCs.

authors:
  - family-names: "De Meyer"
    given-names: "Victorien"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6566-2716"
    affiliation: >-
      Centre pour l'Étude et la Simulation du Climat à l'Échelle Régionale
      (ESCER), Université du Québec à Montréal, Montréal, Québec, Canada

version: "2.0.0-revised"
date-released: "2026-11-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/VictorienDeMeyer/DeMeyer_etal_2026_Extreme_Cyclones"
# Concept DOI of the Zenodo archive: it always resolves to the latest version.
# Each release also gets its own version DOI (v1.0.0-submitted: 10.5281/zenodo.21922111).
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21922110"

keywords:
  - extratropical cyclones
  - extreme precipitation
  - wind extremes
  - regional climate modelling
  - cyclone phase space
  - CRCM6-GEM5
  - ERA5
  - IMERG
  - North America

# GitHub displays this article citation in the "Cite this repository" box.
# Update it (journal, volume, pages, DOI) once the article is published in Weather and Climate Dynamics.
